pixx Path B desktop: X11 compiled entirely by pxx (x86-64)

All of these were compiled and linked by pxx. They are static and use pxx's
own C runtime (crtl), not glibc:

  /usr/bin/Xfbdev        TinyX 1.3 kdrive framebuffer X server (/dev/fb0),
                         plus pixx's patch tinyx-us-keymap-fallback: a built-in
                         US keymap when the console has no KDGKBENT (Rainyday)
  /usr/bin/dwm           dwm 6.5 window manager
  /usr/bin/st            st 0.9.2 terminal
  /usr/bin/startx-pixx   starts Xfbdev, then st, then dwm (busybox sh)
  /usr/bin/lvgl-demo     LVGL 9.2.2 widgets demo, no X: run it on the console
                         as root; it draws straight into /dev/fb0.
                         LV_EVDEV=/dev/input/eventN gives it a pointer, and
                         LV_FBDEV picks another framebuffer.

Libraries linked in: libX11 1.8.10, libxcb 1.17, libXau, libXext, libXrender,
libXft 2.3.8, fontconfig 2.15, freetype 2.13.3, expat 2.6.4, libXfont 1.5.4,
libfontenc 1.1.8 and zlib 1.3.1.

Checked: in QEMU on pixx's Linux kernel (bochs DRM fbdev, 1280x800), this
stack's screenshot is pixel-identical to the same clients on a gcc-built
Xfbdev. pxx's dwm and st are also pixel-identical to gcc's builds on a gcc
Xfbdev, and keyboard input reaches st.

Needs from the system:
- /dev/fb0, plus devtmpfs and devpts (startx-pixx mounts devpts if missing).
- Root: Xfbdev takes over the VT.
- An /etc/passwd entry for the user (st calls getpwuid), with /bin/sh.
- For a mouse: CONFIG_INPUT_MOUSEDEV (+ _PSAUX) in the kernel. Xfbdev reads
  /dev/input/mice or /dev/psaux. The keyboard comes from the VT, which works
  as is.

Licences: see this directory. TinyX's repository is GPLv3 as a whole, but its
source files carry the original X11/MIT notices (owner's call, 2026-11-18).
Everything else is MIT/X11, the FreeType licence, zlib, expat (MIT), or the
DejaVu font licence; misc-fixed is public domain.
